Falsifies `jh7110_entropy::verification::ready_requires_rand_rdy_and_carries_the_words_untouched`.

The defect: `assemble` lays each `RAND` word out big-endian instead of little-endian, so
every four bytes of entropy handed to the caller are byte-reversed. On a JH7110 the
register file is little-endian and this is the plausible slip; the bytes still look
random, so nothing downstream can notice.

Expected to fail: the byte-by-byte comparison against `(rand[i / 4] >> (8 * (i % 4)))`,
on the first word whose bytes are not palindromic.

**Measured blind before the harness was rewritten.** It used to assert
`interpret(istat, rand) == Outcome::Ready(assemble(rand))`, and `interpret` fills that
variant by calling `assemble`, so both sides moved together and this diff left the
harness verifying. Confirmed by applying the diff against the old phrasing and watching
it stay green. The rewrite states the little-endian layout the doc comment claims,
indexed out of the words by hand, which the implementation does not get to choose.

diff --git a/crates/jh7110_entropy/src/lib.rs b/crates/jh7110_entropy/src/lib.rs
index 9905f92e..5aa771cf 100644
--- a/crates/jh7110_entropy/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/jh7110_entropy/src/lib.rs
@@ -291,7 +291,7 @@ pub fn interpret(istat: u32, rand: [u32; 8]) -> Outcome {
 pub fn assemble(rand: [u32; 8]) -> [u8; 32] {
     let mut out = [0u8; 32];
     for (i, word) in rand.iter().enumerate() {
-        out[i * 4..i * 4 + 4].copy_from_slice(&word.to_le_bytes());
+        out[i * 4..i * 4 + 4].copy_from_slice(&word.to_be_bytes());
     }
     out
 }
